{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: the combined R4 site address window, R10 application host trust and R13 unread plaintext expiry delivery at exact head 3447b3b2b17d793e2215a3518b2ffdd1e7d92f6b, tree 96bc114c4374f56a87b744a068185b413ac0cfd6 (review request 3b58e498, promise 89cedab6; common implementation request 7dfe31c9 with promise 4042361e, R4 a4ba3fbc with promise a914d7ea, R10 6123e618 with promise 1fc9f58d, R13 ab2efe97 with promise 0e27a687). One finding is required, and it is about gate evidence, not code: the ordinary gate has not passed at the code tree this head carries. The code review below found no defect and stands for the same tree, so the resubmission needs the gate run, the note update and a new head, nothing more. This review binds all four implementation requests to the candidate so the bindings carry to the resubmission. Do not land this head.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-24-security-common-delivery.md (artifact b93d1651), first and in full, then the three per-request notes (site address window 8b0fd641, install application trust ee161eb5, read token expiry 4d6de232) and the 66 other artifacts at this head; the three duplicate artifact events (58116d41, 7ac10258, 65af8e7c) are the same notes filed under their own requests. The worktree /private/tmp/artroom-builder-security-r4-r10-r13-union-20261024 is clean at the head on branch builder/security-r4-r10-r13-union-20261024. The merge-base with Main is Main itself, 56de7567 (the R9 landing); the head is 31 commits above it. The delta against Main is 67 files, 1,428 insertions and 135 deletions, and the act log holds a pointer at this head for every one of them.\n\nRequired finding (1 of 1): run the ordinary gate once at the repaired code tree and resubmit.\n- Ordinary gate 70364 ran at 83807bb9 and failed in two cases. Both failures were caused by this delivery: the new credential expiry wake read the clock eagerly in the object constructor, which consumed a scripted reading in compose.test.ts:214; and the new site-read admission made the unknown-index fixture in scope-index.test.ts:49 answer 503 instead of 404. The failed run is retained honestly, with its audit.\n- The repair commits 06e44409 and 6192c115 changed production source, credential-store.ts (15 lines) and object.ts (4 lines), two test files, and the seal map. The validated tree is d6e12d17; the final head 3447b3b2 differs from it only in the common note.\n- What has run at d6e12d17: both Scope compilers, 26 cases in five files (continuation 70072), and the Node and Counting tails (26900). What has not: the other 494 Vitest files, about 3,530 cases by subtraction from the failed run's 3,556 terminal identities.\n- docs/testing.md:432 says to run the gate once at the head you will send. The two exceptions (docs/testing.md:597-601) cover a commit that changes only documents, and a repair that changes only the document a failing test reads; neither covers a repair of production source. The request asks me to judge the combined evidence under docs/testing without a whole-suite repeat; under docs/testing the answer is that a source repair after a failed gate needs a new exact-head gate.\n- The rule matters here beyond its letter. The whole run is what found both defects; nothing in the five continuation files would have found a third. The repaired constructor and alarm path in object.ts runs for every scope object in every scope test, and the lazy clock changes when the clock is read for any object with pending expiry work. The 26 cases prove the two repairs do what they say; they do not prove the rest of the suite still passes.\n- To resolve: run `npm run gate` once at 6192c115 or at a note-only descendant of it (tree d6e12d17, or the final tree if the note is updated first), file the gate result with its audit, update the common note's status and the per-request notes' pending lines, and send the new head for review with the same four bindings. If that gate passes, I expect to approve without further code change. If it fails, the same rule applies to whatever is repaired.\n\nConditions of a4ba3fbc (R4 site window), each checked in source:\n1. Only the /site per-address window: SiteReadWindow (packages/scope/src/site/window.ts) admits 240 reads per address per fixed 60-second window, bounds the address to 64 UTF-8 bytes, keeps at most 4,096 keys, sends a missing address to one shared missing bucket, never evicts a live window, and reclaims only expired slots; a backward clock cannot reset a window; a NaN time is refused. site-window.test.ts proves each of these.\n2. Admission before any downstream work: site/route.ts asks the fixed SCOPE_INDEX object (admitServing with kind site-read) after the method, path and query refusals and before any redirect, room, publication or provider call. site-route.test.ts proves zero target and provider calls after a 429 (admissions 241, targets unchanged, fetches 0), the retry-after 60 and no-store headers, the 429 on the bare site root, and 503 when the index binding is missing or throws.\n3. Address policy: only the two Worker entry points (packages/scope/src/worker.ts and apps/room/fetch.ts) pass the platform's cf-connecting-ip; a cf-connecting-ip header on the request itself cannot choose a bucket (the test's synthetic header lands in the same bucket). Site reads do not spend the key or creation windows, and separate stubs reach the same fixed owner (serving-admission.test.ts). The note documents the memory-only counters and the shared missing bucket.\n4. Separate from token and trust work: yes; the only shared files are the Worker entry points and the fixtures.\n\nConditions of 6123e618 (R10 install trust), each checked in source:\n1. Trust before fetch: McpService's trustForBuild runs before the package transport and again after the custody lock (hosted-tools.test.ts shows the second check refusing when the list changes during the await). resolve.ts checks before opening the Git source. The CLI and Node entry take the list from APPLICATION_TRUST; the hosted agent receives it through the McpAgent ports from the Worker environment (application-install-custody.test.ts drives a real enrolled agent from the environment to the opener and sees exactly one package request, with manual redirect and no authorization header).\n2. Exact host confinement: a trust row is exactly {release, publisher, host}; canonicalHost refuses anything the immutable URI parser would not accept as a bare host, so uppercase, a path or a port is refused at parse time. applicationTrustForSource matches https://\u003chost\u003e/ as a prefix of the source remote, with the trailing slash, so a longer host, a userinfo trick or another scheme never matches; a redirect is never followed.\n3. Shared parser alignment: readApplicationTrust in packages/client/src/application-trust.ts is the one parser; scope admission.ts re-exports it, and the content store, screen route, resolver and MCP service consume its rows. A row without host, that is any pre-change list, is refused as invalid.\n4. One meaningful no-fetch witness and control: hosted-tools.test.ts (four refusals with zero requests), application-resolve.test.ts (host mismatch refused before open), and the hosted trust control 5743 (removing the Worker's trust forwarding leaves application-install-custody.test.ts:354 red with the other seven cases passing).\n5. Settings and redeploy steps recorded: the install trust note and wrangler.grow.jsonc (its row gains host github.com); the room wrangler comment names the three fields.\n\nConditions of ab2efe97 (R13 unread plaintext expiry), each checked in source:\n1. Delete unread read-token plaintext at the token's end: credential-store.ts adds private_read_credential (the nonsecret marker for an own mint-read), private_credential_expiry with its due index, and the private_credential_expiry_backfill singleton; triggers keep the queue in step with inserts, drops and deletes. expire(now) clears at most 64 legacy rows and 64 due plaintexts per call. read-token.test.ts runs the real alarm after a restart and shows the unread token dropped at its end while a later token is retained, the journal unchanged, and the dropped token answered 403.\n2. Existing bounded maintenance owner: expiryWake joins the Wakes arbitration in object.ts, scheduled after the activation diagnostic; the alarm calls expire once per activation. The order control 39886 leaves archive-operator.test.ts:224 red when the wake precedes the diagnostic.\n3. 24-hour lifetime and native authority preserved: the mint path and the hours bound are unchanged; a read one second before the end still answers 200.\n4. Partial legacy policy: the backfill classifies a legacy row only through its explicit read marker or its retained proof of an own mint-read (#legacyRead); unknown legacy write rows keep their plaintext, and destination-host.test.ts shows a write credential whose provider id imitates a read handle survives expire. The provider token is not revoked; the note says so.\n5. Source plan accepted before implementation: the note records the analysis order and the Root acceptances.\n\nConditions of 7dfe31c9 (common delivery): the union carries the three slices on the approved R9 Main (the R9 merge 2e001864 changed only the R9 note); the composed Worker and object seals were produced, and the repaired object seal replaces the stale one; the two pending R13 controls (exact end 84265, order 39886) were run and audited. The condition \"one final ordinary gate\" is the one not met: the gate ran and failed, and no gate has run at the repaired tree. That is the required finding above.\n\nCode conclusions. R4: the window is pure and bounded, and refusing when the table is full of live windows is the right failure mode for a quota (filling the table denies new addresses for at most one window and evicts nobody). Site admission lives in the same fixed object as the key and creation windows, so a Worker has one admission owner and a fresh environment object cannot reset it. R10: the host is bound at parse time, so a bad list fails the whole list, which is the right shape for an operator setting; the recheck after custody closes the await gap. R13: the queue holds id and end only, never plaintext; the marker is nonsecret, a fault after marker creation leaves an exact binding that a different mint cannot reuse (conflict) and the same mint can complete later (stored). The lazy clock in expiryWake restores the pre-R13 reading sequence for every object with an empty queue, which is the cheapest correct repair of the compose failure. The seal test run once at the head passes 4 of 4, which computes the parser values for every sealed file.\n\nEvidence verified. Failed gate 70364 (manifest 7 of 7 files, every sha256 verified): head 83807bb9, typecheck exit 0, Vitest 3,551 passed, 2 failed, 3 skipped, 0 unhandled errors. Repair continuation 70072 (manifest 1 of 1): checkpoint 6192c115, tree d6e12d17, 26 cases in five files, complete callbacks. Tail 26900 (manifest 3 of 3): Node 39 of 39, Counting 55 of 55, before and after vectors equal, Main and origin 56de7567. Closed-validation audit (manifest 5 of 5). Controls: all four mutation needles are present in the head's files; the hosted trust and site address controls' original digests equal the head's worker.ts; the exact-end and order controls' original digests are the pre-repair credential-store.ts and object.ts, as the note says, and their needle lines are unchanged by the repair. The four Root acceptances (ef616526, 7eeca2ac, 8ae7d171, cda98615) are in the act log. I ran no suite beyond the seal test and the document checks; the gate is the builder's to run.\n\nDocument checks at 3447b3b2:\n- git diff --check from Main 56de7567 is clean;\n- check-commit-messages from 56de7567 reports 30 checked, 0 flagged; the longest subject is 67 characters;\n- section citations on an export report 1,877 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved; the citation script is identical to Main's;\n- merging the head into Main 56de7567 gives exactly its tree, 96bc114c;\n- the common note's status paragraph is a true dated report: it says the ordinary gate remains failed and claims no whole-gate success. Once the gate has run, that paragraph must say so, and the three per-request notes' pending lines with it.\n\nArchitecture: three slices on one fixed admission owner, one trust parser and one alarm owner; each slice adds to an existing mechanism rather than a new one, and the common delivery keeps request-specific notes and bindings. Right shape.\n\nSecurity: R4 buckets by the platform address only, shares one bucket for unaddressed callers, and loses its counters on eviction; those limits are documented and acceptable for a read quota. R10 closes the fetch-before-trust gap and the host alias gap; the one residual is operational, that an unmigrated list refuses every install until it is rewritten (deploy note below). R13 removes unread plaintext at its end without touching write custody or provider state; the backfill reads only own markers, so it cannot misclassify a write token. No new entry point, secret or provider call is introduced.\n\nSimplification, nonblocking:\n- object.ts swallows every error from expire with an empty catch, and a legacy row whose key exceeds the metadata bound makes expire throw before the backfill cursor advances, so the backfill would stall at that row on every alarm while the due queue keeps working (credential-store.test.ts asserts exactly this). A bounded console diagnostic, or stepping the cursor past the offending key, would make the stall visible. No current mint path can write such a row.\n- The scope-index fixture's scripted admitServing is a stand-in and says so; the real fixed object is covered by serving-admission.test.ts.\n- The site route takes the address as a fourth positional string; a named option would make the three callers easier to read. Cosmetic.\n\nDeploy notes for the planner, for when the resubmitted head lands:\n- APPLICATION_TRUST: every deployed list must carry host on every row before or in the same deploy, or the parser refuses the whole list and installs and screen routes refuse. wrangler.grow.jsonc already has host github.com; the demo and lending files carry no list, so lists set outside the files (dashboard or --var) need the same rewrite.\n- SCOPE_INDEX: a Worker that serves /site/ without the binding answers 503 for every site read. All four room configurations bind it.\n- R13 runs its first backfill through alarms in 64-row quanta on each storage object's next activation; expired read tokens lose their plaintext, provider tokens are not revoked.\n\nFindings: one required, above. Verdict changes-requested.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b93d16511edcd9ecf3725e1d144baaa3778c43a7","binding":"assigned","head":"3447b3b2b17d793e2215a3518b2ffdd1e7d92f6b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:89cedab6fc19f0643f74ce8096882b00f03bb877\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4042361eb7c6096fad91932c17fa7392f7ed704f\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a914d7ea8cb90ff35dee7d0439194e6083778e43\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1fc9f58ddb9211047af3feb263e1f6432d2824e7\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0e27a687c4836c8616ac0307c391dec3a9b99db1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:efe90946c13a3662d45f04e7703820c86d49470a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}